From 88f489e4208c193afbc27f2a25254bfecf80b953 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NeoTech <andreas@roughedge.se>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 00:36:06 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] fix(motion): read a short turned bar's axis off its
 facing flats

The axis fit refused any part whose normal moment is isotropic (both
smaller eigenvalues past ISO = 0.5 of the largest) as "every axis
fits". A turned bar at 0.5 < L/D < 2 balances its end caps against its
walls and sits in that window, so an r10 x 40 bar with one modelled
radial hole was refused by every lathe cross and axial drill.

The fit now also offers the part's flat poles as candidate axes
(axis_poles.rs): sign-folded normals one cluster of facets shares with
at least 5 % of the area, at most three. They are judged by the same
concurrency and coarseness gates. When the plain line about a pole is
dragged by an off-centre feature, it is re-centred by least trimmed
squares first. An isotropic part that no pole earns is still refused
by name: a sphere has no flat, and a cube's faces fail the coarseness
gate. Eigen candidates are unchanged and win ties, so an even revolve
keeps its bits.

Pinned by a_short_punched_bar_reads_as_turned_about_its_axis
(drilling_cross_spindle) and the axis_poles unit tests (L/D 2 and
L/D 1.5 bars fit; a cube and a sphere are refused by name).

Fixes #152

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
