From 2fe41a2bd69a2d877235efeb96347db9b338112e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NeoTech <andreas@roughedge.se>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 01:04:06 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] fix(motion): normal lines through one point no longer
 certify an axis

The axis judgement counted a facet as an inlier when its normal line
met the candidate axis. Two-triangle faces anchor their lines at the
face centre and a single-band wall anchors its lines at the bar's mid
point, so a box's faces (or a single-band shaft's wall) meet EVERY line
through that point. A small finely meshed boss or cross hole then
supplied the bearings, and the fit trusted a wrong axis: a plate with
an r5 boss at (10, 0) read a 45-degree axis, a single-band r10 h150
shaft with a D-flat and a cross hole read X, and (on PR #204) a 60 mm
cube with an off-centre boss read a tilted axis through its centre.

New axis_point.rs: find the point the inliers' lines meet best (area
least squares, refitted on the area half that misses it least). When
the lines through it carry half the inlier area, those facets alone
must pass the coarseness gate about the axis - a pencil of lines has no
direction of its own and stands for a revolve only when it is itself
rotationally symmetric about the axis. A single-band CAD cylinder still
fits; banded walls are untouched. The bearing-gap gate moves into the
new module, shared. On an isotropic part a pole (facing-flat) axis is
no longer tilted by the direction refinement.

Pins (each fails on the base 7fff05c):
a_plate_with_a_boss_off_its_centre_line_is_refused_by_name,
a_single_band_shaft_with_a_flat_and_a_cross_hole_is_not_read_across,
a_cube_with_an_off_centre_boss_is_refused_by_name (1 and 5 bands);
a_single_band_bar_still_fits_its_true_axis guards the common export.

Fixes #208

