From 1a42edf77bd4582a1cb392bd63b9e70ecfe281e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NeoTech <andreas@roughedge.se>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 01:43:41 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] fix(motion): a pencil's rings keep one latitude; flats
 ride with a ring

Second re-review of PR #223:
- B1: a single-band Z shaft crossed at the origin by a rod tilted
  5..45 degrees read the rod's axis, or a line between the two, as
  trusted. Seen from a tilted axis the shaft's wall is a squashed ring:
  its bearings stay spread, so the per-pencil bearing moments read it at
  only (1 - cos ang)/2.
- B2 (regression): a square plate with a centred bore was refused: its
  sides sit in the bore's pencil, and their four bearings broke the cap.

Each pencil is now judged by latitude (n . u), the angle at which a
revolve's ring meets its axis. The facets are sorted into latitude
classes, and each narrow class must hold a ring (bearing skip within
twice the sagitta budget). Flats at the ring's latitude ride with it:
a D-flat, a keyway floor, the plate's sides round its bore. A 90-degree
cross folds onto two bearings at many latitudes and holds none. And the
latitude must not run with the bearing: the share of its variance
explained by the first two bearing harmonics (trigonometric regression)
counts as off the axis once the swing passes 2e-3. Both are capped
together at 10 % of the inlier area. The bearing-moment test is gone.

Pins:
- a_skewed_cross_never_reads_the_rod_or_a_line_between (5/15/30/45 deg,
  rod h60 and h100, 1 and 5 bands): fails on e883ae6.
- a_square_plate_with_a_centred_bore_reads_its_axis (r20 t10, r40 t10,
  r45 t30): fails on e883ae6.

Fixes #208

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
